00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:03.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Google Cloud's
Anthos helps support companies

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.490
in modernizing their services
in hybrid and multi-cloud

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.353
environments.

00:00:06.353 --> 00:00:08.520
But what does the stack
actually look like, and what

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.948
use cases does it solve?

00:00:09.948 --> 00:00:12.240
Join us as we talk to Arctiq
about how they implemented

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.920
a hybrid cloud environment on
this episode of "Stack Chat."

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.790
Thanks so much for
joining us, Kyle.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.880
Tell us a little bit more
about what Arctiq does.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.588
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah,
thanks for having us.

00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:26.270
Arctiq is focused on services,
and we're born in cloud,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.740
and focusing on
helping developers

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.350
using Git code in production,
and all the things that

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.160
go around that.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.618
And most of the time, we're
spending that with enterprises,

00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:36.990
so helping them solve
that culture condition

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.290
problem of moving from
their old world to cloud.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.960
And all this automation and
everything that comes with it--

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.460
we're helping enable them and
getting them to be successful.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.377
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
tell us a little bit more

00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:48.060
about the specific
infrastructure.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.018
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah, so
this particular customer

00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:52.940
we worked with, they have
a traditional application.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.770
They've made it a microservices.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:55.580
It's cloud native.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.210
It runs in GCP and GKE.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.640
And it's very
successful in that,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.280
but they've got
customers that have

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.360
challenges around locality, data
residency, things like that.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.220
So they may have to
deploy in a region

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.130
where there isn't a Google Cloud
or they don't have good network

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:10.980
connectivity.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.890
So for them, they've
had to manage

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.560
that in the past of deploying
homegrown Kubernetes

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.530
environments in those places
and trying to manage them,

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.510
and trying to keep them
updated, and patched.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.100
And it's a tough job to be
able to manage these things.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.193
So they were looking for a
common experience and a way

00:01:26.193 --> 00:01:28.610
to take all the automation and
all the good things they've

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.560
done in cloud, and
be able to apply that

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.940
into their on-prem offering.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.940
And most importantly, offer
a consistent experience that

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.230
is easy to support,
and then the customer

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.760
is not getting a lackluster
experience just because it's

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.780
not in cloud anymore.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.373
So that's really what's
given them the agility

00:01:44.373 --> 00:01:45.290
to be able to do this.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.170
MARK MIRCHANDANI: OK.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.267
So if they've got their
home base in the cloud,

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.350
but then they've got all
these different customers

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.920
in different places, and there's
no commonality between some

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.810
of those environments,
then, as you said,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.580
it's a pretty difficult
thing to manage.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.790
I hear that Anthos kind of
played a big part in helping

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.148
standardize this environment.

00:02:03.148 --> 00:02:04.690
So what does that
actually look like?

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:04.970
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.220
So this customer was
leveraging homegrown Kubernetes

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.100
in many cases, and when they
saw Anthos, their interest was,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.980
could we take Anthos and
that reference architecture,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.780
apply it into our
customer scenarios,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.600
but leverage our exact
same CI/CD pipeline,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.760
all our same Helm
templates, all those charts

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.550
that we've been leveraging
and deploy on there

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.730
without having to replatform
all their deployment every time?

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.803
So they looked at that
as a real game-changer

00:02:27.803 --> 00:02:29.720
for them, to be able to
go to market and solve

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.095
some of these
customer challenges

00:02:31.095 --> 00:02:32.840
that they hadn't been
able to solve yet.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.810
So we started the project
and built the architecture

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.410
around this.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:40.160
We deployed a VMware
cluster in Berlin

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.460
and got that up and running.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.690
And then it was taking all
the lessons learned in cloud.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.390
So they were using Cloud Build.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.480
They were using GCR.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.060
They were using a lot of
those rapid cloud tools.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.880
So really, all we had to do
is get those artifacts now

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.995
into their private stack, and be
able to deploy against Anthos.

00:02:57.995 --> 00:03:00.620
So within a couple of weeks, we
were able to stand all this up,

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.240
put it through
performance testing.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.530
It was a real lesson learned.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:07.550
And the biggest impact was, can
we take this common experience

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.680
and make this on-prem
deployment look just

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.180
like another deployment region?

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.380
That was the real goal.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:12.680
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.080
So now, they've got all
of these environments that

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.570
are kind of like offshoots
of their core environment,

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.397
but they all look
pretty much the same.

00:03:19.397 --> 00:03:21.230
And then they roll out
the code the same way

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.897
they were kind of
rolling it out before,

00:03:22.897 --> 00:03:25.200
but now it's easy to roll
it out to all of those.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:25.340
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.800
It really is that perfect
hybrid story because that's

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.380
the deployment region,
is the end-state code

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.280
after it's tested.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.600
That's where it's going to run.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.320
But they still have to do
innovation and testing,

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.507
and put new features in.

00:03:37.507 --> 00:03:39.590
So that's where the cloud
comes in handy for them.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.170
So they were able to spin
up clusters on demand,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.050
and we started looking at things
like config management for them

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.900
and helping them Gitify
all that workflow.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.540
But they can spin up
clusters on demand in GKE,

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.730
do all their testing,
tear it back down.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.310
Once they get a release ready,
they can push that release out

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.350
to that on-prem environment,
and it's all just

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.120
through standard
automation framework.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.840
So it takes away the whole
worrying about a maintenance

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.400
window, or doing it on Sunday.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.520
They can roll out changes and
be really rapid and really agile

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.060
in their development cycle.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.727
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
even though they've

00:04:10.727 --> 00:04:12.500
kind of made all the
other environments

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.960
common to their
home environment,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.462
how do they keep them in sync?

00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:18.170
KYLE BASSETT: Well,
keeping them in sync,

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:19.519
one of the big things
we've been looking at

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:21.932
is doing everything through
policy, so policy management.

00:04:21.932 --> 00:04:23.390
And that's one of
the things config

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.830
management, part
of Anthos, brings

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.160
to the table is instead of
people going in and making

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.830
manual changes to
clusters, why isn't

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.680
it done through a Git workflow,
or PR process and a peer

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:34.310
review?

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.070
And when that gets approved,
it gets pushed out.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.440
And not just to one cluster.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.980
You can do that
to many clusters.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.250
You can also do it to
clusters on-prem or in cloud.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.900
The other piece it
gives them is they

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.090
can add extra things that
they're concerned about,

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:51.560
like security frameworks, or
if they're doing custom Istio

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.080
rule sets, their developers--
they can start to make sure

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.150
that that framework is spread
across all the environments.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.300
And then that steps up the
audit capability, the compliance

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:00.800
side.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.925
So when someone
comes in and says,

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:03.410
how are you managing
these things?

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:04.820
They're able to build
a report and say,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.140
this is all driven from source.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.598
Look at the Git
log, and that's how

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:08.806
we manage our infrastructure.

00:05:08.806 --> 00:05:10.431
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And it's all the same

00:05:10.431 --> 00:05:12.830
across all those environments
because the unified config

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:14.372
management makes it
easy to roll out.

00:05:14.372 --> 00:05:16.538
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah, even
down to if someone jumps on

00:05:16.538 --> 00:05:17.970
with an elevated
access and makes

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.700
a manual change to a cluster,
that drift that usually

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:21.900
happens?

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.800
Config Manager will
change it back.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.220
But if you put time into
building the policies, and then

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.500
developers can focus
on writing code,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.870
and the security teams
can focus on making sure

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.440
that that code is
of the quality.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.970
So if everyone puts
time into this,

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:35.970
it just comes
through a pipeline,

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.980
and it all drives the quality.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.140
And people in
various departments,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.790
and most importantly,
developers can

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.998
focus on developing and
releasing at a high velocity.

00:05:43.998 --> 00:05:45.540
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And their customers

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.180
just get to focus on using
the same stable application

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.930
that now they feel more
confident in because the parent

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.218
company kind of has that
responsibility of making

00:05:54.218 --> 00:05:55.260
sure it works everywhere.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:55.560
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.030
In the end, the
customer just wants

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.440
to be able to do their job, and
they're buying the software.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.780
And they want to know that
they have the trust that it's

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.640
going to be up,
because a lot of these

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.270
are billing-based application.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.030
It's like the revenue-generating
side of the business.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:12.150
So they're looking at
it as a managed service,

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.860
but they've got constraints
that they have to be on-prem.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.045
But they need to know that when
new packages or new updates

00:06:18.045 --> 00:06:19.920
come, they don't have
that worry that they're

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.830
going to have to go through
a lot of pain or an outage

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.437
to their customer, which
isn't good, obviously.

00:06:25.437 --> 00:06:28.020
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So as you were
kind of helping them roll out

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.060
this entire architecture,
what would you

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.393
say kind of the biggest hurdle
that you jumped over was?

00:06:32.393 --> 00:06:34.227
KYLE BASSETT: I would
say just standardizing

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:35.370
all the configuration.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.080
This particular
customer spent a lot

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.910
of time putting the work in.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.088
And knowing that they
could do it manually

00:06:41.088 --> 00:06:42.630
to get it working
once, they actually

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.520
put the time into
automating it all

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.560
and testing it,
which really pays off

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.960
in folds as you start to scale.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.490
So that was one
of the challenges

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.920
we took is just getting that
exact workflow they were

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.960
using in cloud and
applying it and finding

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.542
the pipeline for on-prem.

00:06:56.542 --> 00:06:58.500
Obviously there were some
security restrictions

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.840
around networking and access.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.670
We had to deploy
an on-prem registry

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.650
and find a way to sync
all the images down

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.417
so we have that workflow.

00:07:06.417 --> 00:07:08.250
So the biggest thing
is they put the time in

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.050
and we worked with
them to enhance it,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.810
and then now they're paying off
in folds as they roll this out.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.123
As they move forward to push
this and scale their business,

00:07:16.123 --> 00:07:17.790
they don't have a lot
of technical debt.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.920
It's all documentation
is living.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.690
It's all in the Git repo.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thanks so much
for coming in and showing us

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.410
how you actually kind of
built this architecture

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.797
with this customer to support
all of their own customers.

00:07:28.797 --> 00:07:30.630
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah, so
thanks for having us.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.713
We're really looking forward
to rolling out Anthos

00:07:32.713 --> 00:07:36.347
to more customers and
getting it out in the market.

00:07:36.347 --> 00:07:38.430
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Get
Anthos ready by checking out

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.410
this quick lab on
modernizing and deploying

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.652
your application with GKE.

00:07:42.652 --> 00:07:44.610
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.070
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

